<DocsLayout
  title="Troubleshooting — Apple Docs"
  description="Every message the Apple Studio panel can show, what it means, and the shortest fix — plus the panel that never appeared and the build that stalled."
  heading="Troubleshooting"
>
  <p>
    Grouped by what you can actually see. If the panel is showing you a message, start with
    <a href="#messages">what the panel says</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="messages">What the panel says</h2>

  <h3>“Invalid or expired code”</h3>
  <p>
    Codes last 10 minutes and work once. Generate a fresh one in the web workspace
    (<a href="/docs/connect">Connect a project</a>) and type it in promptly. Case and dashes do not
    matter, so mistyping those is not the cause — a stale code almost always is.
  </p>

  <h3>“Could not reach Apple — check your internet”</h3>
  <p>
    The plugin's HTTPS request never landed. In order of likelihood:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Studio's permission prompt was declined.</strong> Roblox asks once, per plugin, per
      web address. Re-allow it in Studio's <strong>Plugin Management</strong> window, where these
      permissions can be accepted, denied and revoked.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The network blocks it.</strong> School and workplace networks often block outbound HTTPS from Studio. Another network settles it in a minute.</li>
    <li><strong>Apple is actually down.</strong> The <a href="/status">status page</a> checks the live API from your own browser and answers that in a second.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>“Session ended — reconnect with a new code”</h3>
  <p>
    A Studio connection is good for 30 days, then it expires by design.
    <a href="/docs/connect">Connect again</a>; nothing in the project is lost.
  </p>

  <h3>“This Apple build is too old for the server”</h3>
  <p>
    Shown in red, and it does block builds. Update the plugin —
    <a href="/docs/updating">Updating the plugin</a> has the two-click version. This is rare by
    design: Apple admits every plugin build whose wire protocol it still supports, and today it
    supports all of them, so you should not see this yet.
  </p>

  <h3>An amber line about a newer version</h3>
  <p>
    Advisory only. A newer Apple has been published and you are still on an older one — everything
    keeps working. Update when convenient. It is written to Studio's Output window once, not
    repeatedly.
  </p>

  <h3>“Connection hiccup — retrying…”</h3>
  <p>
    One request failed and the plugin is already retrying. It clears itself. If it never clears,
    treat it as “Could not reach Apple” above.
  </p>

  <h2>The Apple button isn't in the Plugins tab</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Acquiring the plugin on the web only puts it in your inventory. The install happens inside
      Studio, in the <strong>Toolbox</strong> — see <a href="/docs/plugin">Install</a>.
    </li>
    <li>Check Studio's plugin management window: if Apple is listed but disabled, enable it.</li>
    <li>Restart Studio once. Plugins are loaded at launch.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The panel says Connected, the web app says it isn't</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A connection belongs to one Studio window.</strong> If you opened a second window or
      a different place, that one is not connected — connect it too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check you are looking at the same project.</strong> The panel names the project it is
      connected to, right next to <strong>Connected</strong>. If that is not the project open in
      your browser, that is the whole answer.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Apple stopped mid-build</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Studio was closed or the place was switched.</strong> Apple loses its hands and pauses
      with a message. Reopen the place, reconnect if needed, and ask it to continue — the plan and
      history are still in the project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Very large places are slow to read.</strong> The first tree read in a session can take
      noticeably longer than the rest. Give it a moment before retrying.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Credits ran out.</strong> The chat says so explicitly. See
      <a href="/docs/credits-and-limits">Credits &amp; limits</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-failures">The model failed part-way</h2>
  <p>
    Three different things are meant by “it stopped on its own”, and they need different responses.
    The chat says which one happened; this is what each means, and what it costs.
  </p>

  <h3>“The model dropped that step”</h3>
  <p>
    A single inference call failed after Apple had already done real work. Everything up to that
    step is saved in your place, and the dropped step is <strong>not</strong> charged — Credits are
    billed from the compute a call actually reports, and a call that returned nothing reports
    nothing. Send another message and Apple continues from where it stopped; you do not need to
    repeat the whole instruction.
  </p>

  <h3>“Apple is busy” or “too many requests”</h3>
  <p>
    The model provider rate-limited us, not you. Nothing about your account changed and your
    allowance is untouched. Wait a few seconds and continue — this clears on its own, and it clears
    faster than retrying immediately, which joins the same queue.
  </p>

  <h3>“Apple has reached today's shared building capacity”</h3>
  <p>
    Different from running out of your own Credits: this is a ceiling on what the whole service will
    spend in a day, and it protects the free tier from a single runaway build. The message says when
    it resets — midnight UTC, the same instant for everyone. Your Credits are not consumed by a run
    that stops here, and your project and checkpoints are exactly where you left them. See
    <a href="/docs/credits-and-limits">Credits &amp; limits</a> for the difference between the two.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      In all three cases the work already done is in your place and the conversation is intact.
      Apple never rolls back finished steps because a later one failed — that is what the checkpoint
      before the run is for, and it is still there if you want it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Apple changed something I didn't want</h2>
  <p>Three exits, smallest first:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> (<kbd>⌘Z</kbd> on macOS) in Studio. Each batch of Apple changes is one native undo step.</li>
    <li>Restore the checkpoint Apple took before the run, from the project's sidebar.</li>
    <li>Say what is wrong in the chat. Fixing a wrong turn is usually faster than rolling back.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Stop Apple now</h2>
  <p>
    Press <strong>Disconnect</strong> in the plugin panel, or disconnect from the web workspace.
    Either one ends Apple's access to the place immediately, without uninstalling anything. To
    remove the plugin entirely, uninstall it from Studio's plugin management window.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Before writing in, check <a href="/status">Status</a> — it lists what is currently known to be
      broken or limited, with the workaround for each, and it says when the API last answered.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Still stuck? Email <a href="mailto:apple.labs.app@gmail.com">apple.labs.app@gmail.com</a> with
      the project name, roughly when it happened, and what the panel said. A human reads it.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
